you know was after Thanksgiving prep  last year and my chef Chris Lim he gets  very excited when he has these ideas and  he kind of just smacked me on the back  we kind of looked me in the eyes and  said yo what about turkey under  cinderblock  you know we've all kind of looked at  each other and what can we do to make  this more fun and you know we're gonna  do a play on our classic dish chicken  under a brick we're just gonna do a  turkey under a cinderblock  you're first gonna get rid of the  wishbone then we're gonna take off the  breasts and then in the same motion  you're gonna take the leg and the thigh  off with the breast and the wing intact  we'll season the flesh with salt black  pepper and a little bit of lemon zest  and then when you flip it over the skin  we only season with salt this is where  you get the pen smoking hot just with a  little bit of canola oil and you really  gotta wait for that smoke because when  you put this 16 pound bird into this pan  the temperatures gonna drop and you're  gonna add each side of the bird skin  side down and then you're gonna almost  kind of interlock them so that it's one  solid piece and then once you do that  very very very carefully you're gonna  take a cinder block wrapped in aluminum  foil and you're gonna kind of press it  down so that you put it at an angle  where it's actually pressing down the  breasts to thighs breasts and at this  point you want to leave it on the heat  until you start to see the brown and  start to form around the edges but  that's when you go into a 500 degree  oven for about 20 to 25 minutes you know  now you're gonna take the turkey out of  the oven then take the cinder block  which is gonna be hot at this point take  it off put that aside and you just let  it sit on the heat until you know you  can check it every once in a while with  a spatula but you want to get like this  almost like glassy kind of skin on the  bottom and then we will flip the turkey  on to its flesh side just for 10 to 15  seconds like literally it's just like a  kiss you're just kind of sealing the  juices in and then we'll put it to that  resting rack and let it rest for 8 to 10  minutes we'll add the turkey back in  we'll throw in handfuls of chopped herbs  and fried herbs and autumn olives and  all this kind of stuff to get everything  nice and bubbly and beautiful and  presentation and then we're gonna take  spoonfuls of this and base the turkey  I'm gonna kind of put everything back  together hit it with fresh herbs and  bring it kind of up to a boil so it's  kind of like sizzling and then when you  bring it to the table you know you'll  have everything bubbling and  and the smell and all that kind of stuff  and bring it to the table hot serve it  in the in the pan that you cooked it in  and you let people get dirty so from all  of us here at restaurant Marc Forgione  Happy Thanksgiving hope to see you soon  and remember to have fun okay
 

